{"role":"assistant","content":"Fixed and verified. The integration had three independent bugs, each matching one symptom in `logs/`:\n\n**Causes**\n1. **Connect button 401'd for everyone** — `static/app.js` built the Connect action as a plain `<a href=\"/api/intervals/auth/init\">`. A full-page navigation carries no `Authorization` header (the SPA token only travels on `fetch`), so the server rejected every athlete before an authorize URL was ever built (log §1, ticket #214).\n2. **The authorize URL itself was rejected** — `config.py`'s scope listed `ACTIVITY` and `CALENDAR` twice each (`:READ` + `:WRITE`). Intervals.icu rejects that with `400 invalid_request` before the consent screen — so even the paste-the-link workaround died when the calendar scope shipped on 2026-10-02 (log §2, ticket #219).\n3. **Good connections expired after exactly an hour** — the token response has no `refresh_token`/`expires_in` (the token lives until revoked), but `store_connection` fabricated a 3600s TTL and the background refresher flipped Rivera to \"expired, reconnect\" an hour later, with nothing to renew it (log §3).\n\n**Fixes**\n- `POST /api/intervals/auth/init` returns `{\"authorize_url\"}` as JSON; the SPA POSTs via `fetch` and then `window.location.assign(...)`. The GET route is gone.\n- `SCOPES = \"ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE\"` — each area once.\n- Tokens stored with no expiry; the refresher is deleted. A 401 from the Intervals API now lazily clears the token and marks \"expired, reconnect\", and reconnecting re-runs the OAuth flow.\n- Token exchange now sends only `client_id`, `client_secret`, `code`.\n\n**Verification** (no external calls — a local stub emulated the documented provider behavior): full connect flow → 302 back → `status: connected` → wellness proxy 200 → simulated revocation flips to \"expired, reconnect\" without crashing → reconnect works → still connected after the wait that used to trigger the fabricated expiry. The stub confirmed the exchange carried exactly the three fields.\n\n`ANSWER.md` documents all of this, including which parts of your colleague's note applied (the POST-init, duplicate-scope, and lazy-401 items — causes 1–3; the standard-lite exchange, aligned though not itself a failure) and which were skipped (JWT `state` with purpose claim — the existing signed/TTL'd state already works; the account-mismatch guard; encryption at rest — the store is in-memory only)."}
